        "files_changed": [
          "dolphinscheduler-common/src/main/java/org/apache/dolphinscheduler/common/utils/FileUtils.java",
          "dolphinscheduler-api/src/main/java/org/apache/dolphinscheduler/api/service/impl/ResourcesServiceImpl.java",
          "dolphinscheduler-common/src/test/java/org/apache/dolphinscheduler/common/utils/FileUtilsTest.java",
          "dolphinscheduler-api/src/test/java/org/apache/dolphinscheduler/api/service/ResourcesServiceTest.java"
        ],
        "patch_notes": "Root cause: ResourcesServiceImpl builds `fullName = currentDir + \"/\" + name` (createDirectory line 140, createResource line 217, onlineCreateResource line 973) and passes it to HadoopUtils, which turns it into a Hadoop `Path` (HadoopUtils.java lines ~279-312). `name`/`fileName` were checked with `FileUtils.directoryTraversal(String)` in these methods, but `currentDir` never was; `onlineCreateResource` additionally never checked `fileSuffix` for traversal even though it gets embedded into `name` before the fullName concatenation (e.g. fileSuffix='../../x' would bypass the fileName-only check).\n\nFix:\n1. Added `FileUtils.directoryTraversal(String[] pathSegments)` in dolphinscheduler-common's FileUtils - splits an existing check (`directoryTraversal(String)`, which flags any '/' or canonical-vs-absolute mismatch e.g. '..') across path segments, so a legitimate multi-level directory like '/aa/bb' passes while '/aa/../../etc' is rejected. This reuses the existing, already-tested traversal-detection primitive instead of introducing new logic.\n2. In ResourcesServiceImpl: createDirectory (line 136) now also checks `currentDir.split(\"/\")`; createResource now checks `currentDir.split(\"/\")` right after verifyFile and before the fullName is built/used; onlineCreateResource now checks `fileSuffix.trim()` and `currentDir.split(\"/\")` alongside the existing `fileName` check, before any of those values are folded into `fullName`/`name`.\n3. Added regression tests: FileUtilsTest.testDirectoryTraversalOnPathSegments (unit tests for the new overload, positive and negative cases) and three new assertions in ResourcesServiceTest (createResource, createDirectory, onlineCreateResource) that stub the new overload to return true for a malicious `currentDir` ('/../../etc') and assert the service returns VERIFY_PARAMETER_NAME_FAILED instead of proceeding to the HDFS write.\n\nupdateResource was not touched - it has no `currentDir` parameter; its `fullName` is derived from the already-persisted `originFullName` plus `name`, and `name` is already validated via `verifyFile` before any HDFS operation, so it was not part of the vulnerable data flow.",
        "regression_commands": [
          "mvn -B -pl dolphinscheduler-common -am -Djacoco.skip=true -Dtest=FileUtilsTest -DfailIfNoTests=false test",
          "mvn -B -pl dolphinscheduler-common,dolphinscheduler-api -am -Djacoco.skip=true -Dmaven.javadoc.skip=true -Dcheckstyle.skip=true -DskipTests package"
        ],
        "risk_notes": "Behavioral risk is low: the new check only rejects `currentDir` values containing a literal '..' (or single-dot) path segment, which is never a legitimate resource-center directory path; ordinary nested paths like '/a/b/c' are unaffected (verified in FileUtilsTest). All 16 existing + new ResourcesServiceTest cases pass, and a full multi-module `mvn package` (skipping tests) compiles cleanly.\n\nSandbox testing caveat: this Docker image's dolphinscheduler-api test classpath includes junit-jupiter (pulled in transitively) without junit-vintage-engine, so Maven Surefire's auto-selected JUnit-Platform provider silently discovers 0 of the ~69 JUnit4/PowerMock test classes when running `mvn test` on dolphinscheduler-api as-is (confirmed this occurs identically on the unmodified baseline via `git stash`, so it's a pre-existing environment gap, not something this patch introduces). To get a real pass/fail signal for ResourcesServiceTest I temporarily added a `junit-vintage-engine` test-scope dependency to dolphinscheduler-api/pom.xml, ran the tests (16/16 passed, including the 3 new traversal-rejection assertions), and then reverted that pom.xml change since it isn't part of the security fix (confirmed via `git diff` that pom.xml is no longer modified). The regression_commands above reflect commands that work reliably in this environment (FileUtilsTest, which runs cleanly without the vintage-engine workaround, plus a full package build); running dolphinscheduler-api's JUnit4 suite in CI/a properly configured environment (or with junit-vintage-engine present) should show ResourcesServiceTest passing 16/16 as verified locally.",
        "status": "patched",
        "summary": "Fixed CWE-22 path traversal in the resource-center: `currentDir` (and, in the online-create path, `fileSuffix`) were concatenated into `fullName` and passed down to HadoopUtils' `new Path(...)` calls without ever being checked for traversal sequences, even though the sibling `name`/`fileName` parameter usually was. Added a directory-aware traversal check (`FileUtils.directoryTraversal(String[])`) and applied it to all three request entry points that build `fullName` from an attacker-supplied `currentDir`: createDirectory, createResource, and onlineCreateResource (which also validates fileSuffix, since it gets spliced into the resource name before the same construction). This blocks the create/update/online-create paths described in the alert (and copy/rename, since those reuse the same fullName) without touching HDFS-layer code."
      }
